<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Russell Clerk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir George Clerk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir George Russell Clerk was a British administrator who spent most of his career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, twice serving as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>. In 1853–1854 he was special commissioner for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a>, sent to end British rule there, and on 23 February 1854 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein-convention">Bloemfontein Convention</a>, which recognised the independence of the Orange Free State.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Clerk was born at Worting House in Mortimer West End, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, the son of John Clerk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> and Anne St John Mildmay, a daughter and coheir of Carew St John Mildmay of Shawford House. Like every civil servant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> before competitive examinations were introduced in the 1850s, he was trained at Haileybury College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, and in 1817 he was posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> as a writer. In 1827 he married Mary, the widow of a Colonel Stewart; she died on 26 November 1878.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Most of Clerk's work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> lay in the Political and Secret Department of the government. He served as an assistant to the President in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, then as Political Agent at Ambala, at Ludhiana from 1839 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> from 1840. In 1843 he was made Lieutenant-Governor of the North-Western Provinces, and from 1848 to 1850 he held his first term as Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>.</p><p>His work then took him outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. After helping to establish the Orange Free State, he became the first Permanent Under-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, a post that replaced the Examiner at East India House once the government of India was taken out of the hands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> as governor for a second term, from 11 May 1860 to 24 April 1862.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Like many senior officers of the civil and military services, Clerk was then appointed a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-india">Council of India</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where he sat until 1876. He died at his London home, 33 Elm Park Gardens, on 25 July 1889. His son was Sir Godfrey Clerk, and his grandson Sir George Clerk. The family name was pronounced Clark.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Russell Clerk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Bloemfontein Convention of 23 February 1854, the special commissioner <b>Sir George Clerk</b> handed the territory to representatives of the burghers, some of whom had been reluctant to accept independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
